8 #edb/ref 17592186101787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101635 #edb/ref 1061 1308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101635 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "15f6a8f4-eaac-50a8-afbe-54bb66398200" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101635 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101636 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101636 #edb/ref 1061 1312 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101636 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b7c05933-f26a-5ee0-9876-89e41517c2c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101636 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101771 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101771 #edb/ref 1061 25278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101771 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "538a0daa-f570-5287-b6c1-2fd7946ab4da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101771 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101772 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101772 #edb/ref 1061 25279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101772 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "826cab52-c133-5e02-bcc7-a04d9190cd1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101772 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101784 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101784 #edb/ref 1061 26716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101784 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "21d8a180-a349-5a05-bcdf-9edbed0ff759" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101784 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101787 #edb/ref 1061 27666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101787 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a9290d9e-3fb4-5a08-97b2-eea432a98c6d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101787 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101995 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the function $f(x)$ below:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{x^{2} - 1}{x^{2} + x - 2},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ne 1 \\\\ k,\\end{vmatrix}x = 1\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ seems to have a point discontinuity at $x=1$. However, by selecting a particular value for $k$, we can remove the point discontinuity and make the function continuous.\n\nWe can find this value of $k$ by forcing the limit of the function to be equal to the value of the function at $x=1$:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1} f(x) = f(1)\n$$\n\nRemember that $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1} f(x)$ represents the value that $f(x)$ approaches as $x$ gets closer and closer, but not equal, to $1$. So, when evaluating $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1} f(x)$, we need to use the function definition for\n\n$$\nx \\neq 1\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 1)f(x) &= lim_(x → 1)\\frac{x^{2} - 1}{x^{2} + x - 2} \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 1)((x - 1)(x + 1))/((x - 1)(x + 2)) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 1)((x - 1)(x + 1))/((x - 1)(x + 2)) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 1)\\frac{x + 1}{x + 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 + 1}{1 + 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $x=1$, the function is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=k\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\nf(1)=k\n$$\n\n, and for continuity we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 1)f(x) &= f(1) \\\\\n\\frac{2}{3} &= k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to make $f(x)$ continuous at $x=1$, we need to choose\n\n$$\nk=\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101995 #edb/ref 1065 548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101995 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "81a7db1c-98c9-5308-96d8-457b407671db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101995 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101997 #edb/ref 1064 "The function $f(x)$ is differentiable on an open interval containing $x=2$. The values of $f$ and $f'$ are given in the following table:\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $4$ |\n\nLet's find\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2} \\dfrac{f(x)}{x^3-8}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we try to substitute $x=2$ directly into the limit, we reach the indeterminate form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2)(f(x))/(x^{3} - 8) &= \\left.(f(x))/(x^{3} - 8)\\right|_{x = 2} \\\\\n&= (f(2))/(2^{3} - 8) \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{8 - 8} \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we use L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2)(f(x))/(x^{3} - 8) &= lim_(x → 2)((f(x))^{′})/((x^{3} - 8)^{′}) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 2)(f^{′}(x))/(3x^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=2$ again, and this time, we are able to determine the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2)(f^{′}(x))/(3x^{2}) &= \\left.(f^{′}(x))/(3x^{2})\\right|_{x = 2} \\\\\n&= (f^{′}(2))/(3 \\cdot 2^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3 \\cdot 2^{2} - 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2} \\dfrac{f(x)}{x^3-8}=\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101997 #edb/ref 1065 599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101997 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c8d2f073-f126-5950-9555-61f8a6dd159a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101997 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102004 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we are given the graph of the function $f(x)$, as shown below. The function has critical points at $x=-1$, $x=2$, and an inflection point at $x=0.5$.\nHow can we sketch the graph of the derivative $f'(x)$?\n\n![](images/b3/b3fcc7a08d2a083567cc2de62c9bb0c6e9516552c8a2f08d2e814604f5ec84f8.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have $f'(x)=0$.\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have $f'(x) > 0$.\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have $f'(x) < 0$.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{min}$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n| $f'$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nUsing this information, we can identify the regions where $f'(x)$ should cross the $x$-axis. According to the table, these are points $(-1,0)$ and $(2,0)$:\n\n![](images/e9/e98d21084e06d2a28509b0b68f449c5f42959ee1adad9b3dbf912885968a3b33.png)\n\nAdditionally, we are told that the function has an inflection point at $x = 0.5$. So $f''(0.5)=0$ and the tangent to the graph of $y=f'(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\nWe don't know the exact value of $f'(0.5)$ but from the graph of $y=f(x)$, we can conclude that at $x = 0.5$ the slope is negative. Therefore, we get a point below the $x$-axis:\n\n![](images/59/5994a9139fbd1c530692a2fb40b2fb1b4b7bd0b34b2bf7ff7194390542748977.png)\n\nFinally, connecting up the parts of the graph above, we get an approximate sketch of $f'(x)$:\n\n![](images/f2/f21fca7744d7196e457f0a9d4511f644a3cf1e8c61018c152fc74ea7f6999f3e.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102004 #edb/ref 1065 641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102004 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "732e85fe-aec5-51f6-806c-68e476077346" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102004 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102007 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to sketch a possible graph of a function $f(x)$ that satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- $f'(3)=0$\n- if $x \\in (-\\infty,3)$ then $f'(x)> 0$\n- if $x \\in (3,+\\infty)$ then $f'(x)<0$\n- $f''(x) < 0$ for all $x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$\n\nWe summarize all the given information in the following table:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f'$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f''$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $\\nearrow\\:\\:\\frown$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\searrow\\:\\:\\frown$ |\n\nNow, let's sketch our graph:\n\n- We draw a point on the vertical line $x=3$, where the tangent to the graph is horizontal.\n- Since $x=3$ is a local maximum, the graph must go down to the left and to the right of that point.\n- Finally, the graph must be concave down everywhere.\n\nTherefore, a possible graph of $f$ is as follows:\n\n![](images/7c/7c6d8412e9c657e8e4a148e40ca0b8a073735d3b88681eec6a79f763471aaf4f.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102007 #edb/ref 1065 655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102007 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7f1b863e-e172-5e84-8f8d-1ea21025605d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102007 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102013 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's remind ourselves of the general strategy for optimization problems:\n\n1. Draw a diagram of the situation, introducing variables where necessary.\n2. Write down the equation of the quantity that you're trying to maximize or minimize. This is called the **objective function**, and it might include more than one variable.\n3. Write down the constraint equation.\n4. Use the constraint equation to write the objective function in terms of a single variable only.\n5. Differentiate the objective function, set it equal to zero, and solve for the stationary points.\n6. Test each stationary point using the second derivative test. Or you can use the first derivative test if it's easier.\n\nWe can use differentiation to solve optimization problems involving inscribed shapes. Let's find out how." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102013 #edb/ref 1065 679 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102013 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3b58b0ee-ece2-52dc-8624-cdfc8dcf2fcb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102013 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105810 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105810 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105810 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105810 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105810 #edb/ref 1039 "Evaluating the Right-Sided Limit of a Rational Function at a Vertical Asymptote" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105810 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105810 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2838ed19-abed-560e-8721-09afa1d696ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105811 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] 