 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106807 #edb/ref 1061 31133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106807 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c2857b6c-e2ae-51ac-8fed-6318597083dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106807 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106808 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106808 #edb/ref 1061 32691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106808 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5afd4693-e1ad-5f9f-94cf-364eceabce7d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106808 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106809 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106809 #edb/ref 1061 32711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106809 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e126b8c5-2d87-5d89-bbf3-8265d50ec279" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106809 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107120 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106810 #edb/ref 1061 32713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106810 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0acbbc47-60c9-5d52-bac4-6df37c07940b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106810 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107031 #edb/ref 1064 "Rational functions have infinite limits when the $x$-value approaches vertical asymptotes. To find the infinite limits of a rational function $f(x)$, we first find the vertical asymptotes.\n\nFor example, consider the rational function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x-2}\n$$\n\nThis rational function has a vertical asymptote at $x=2$.\n\nTo find the one-sided limits at the asymptote $x=2$, we check the sign of $f(x)$ as $x$ approaches $2$ from the left and the right.\n\n- To find the limit as $x \\to 2^-$, we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=2$ from the left:\n\n| $x$ | $1.9$ | $1.99$ | $1.999$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $-10$ | $-100$ | $-1\\,000$ |\n\nThe values of $f(x)$ are negative, and they get more and more negative. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} f(x) = -\\infty\n$$\n\n- To find the limit as $x \\to 2^+$, we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=2$ from the right:\n\n| $x$ | $2.1$ | $2.01$ | $2.001$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $10$ | $100$ | $1\\,000$ |\n\nThe values of $f(x)$ are positive, and they get larger and larger. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^+} f(x) = +\\infty\n$$\n\nThese one-sided limits match up with what we see in the graph of $f(x)$, shown below.\n\n![](images/24/248e651214f9339d5e47f09f3eecd3387897eda525d99845258d4466025f16ac.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107031 #edb/ref 1065 1338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107031 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c5a2dd1f-90e1-5bcc-a854-1debabaffe1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107031 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107119 #edb/ref 1064 "$$\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nSince the hyperbolic functions are defined in terms of exponential functions, we can easily calculate their derivatives.\n\nThe derivatives of the basic hyperbolic functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\sinh x) &= \\cosh x \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\cosh x) &= \\sinh x \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\tanh x) &= sech^{2} x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use these results to find the derivative of the following function:\n\n$$\ny = \\sinh{x} - 2\\cosh{x}\n$$\n\nUsing the usual properties of derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sinh x - 2\\cosh x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sinh x) + \\frac{d}{dx}(-2\\cosh x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sinh x) - 2\\frac{d}{dx}(\\cosh x) \\\\\n&= \\cosh x - 2\\sinh x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107119 #edb/ref 1065 5207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107119 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "df316238-1ead-50db-80ec-d47656e6acad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107119 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107120 #edb/ref 1064 "$$\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} %\n$$\n\nWe would like the prove the following result:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\sinh{x}) = \\cosh x\n$$\n\nTo do this, we revert to the definition of the hyperbolic sine:\n\n$$\n\\sinh x = \\dfrac{1}{2}(e^x-e^{-x})\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equation with respect to $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\sinh x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{1}{2}(e^{x} - e^{-x})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(e^{x} - e^{-x}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(e^{x} + e^{-x}) \\\\\n&= \\cosh x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe proof for the derivative of the hyperbolic cosine is very similar. You may wish to try it for yourself." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107120 #edb/ref 1065 5221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107120 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "11e38932-d6a4-5649-bbbe-205fb1bad98d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107120 #edb/ref 1067 "Deriving the Formula for the Derivative of the Hyperbolic Sine" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107121 #edb/ref 1064 "$$\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nWe would like the prove the following result:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(\\tanh{x}) = \\sech^2{x}\n$$\n\nWe do this using the definition of the hyperbolic tangent. The most convenient definition to work with for this purpose is\n\n$$\n\\tanh{x} = \\dfrac{e^{2x}-1}{e^{2x}+1}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equation with respect to $x$ using the quotient rule, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\tanh x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{e^{2x} - 1}{e^{2x} + 1}) \\\\\n&= ((e^{2x} - 1)^{′} \\cdot (e^{2x} + 1) - (e^{2x} - 1) \\cdot (e^{2x} + 1)^{′})/((e^{2x} + 1)^{2}) \\\\\n&= ((2e^{2x})(e^{2x} + 1) - (e^{2x} - 1)(2e^{2x}))/((e^{2x} + 1)^{2}) \\\\\n&= (2e^{4x} + 2e^{2x} - 2e^{4x} + 2e^{2x})/((e^{2x} + 1)^{2}) \\\\\n&= (4e^{2x})/((e^{2x} + 1)^{2}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{2e^{x}}{e^{2x} + 1})^{2} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{2}{e^{x} + e^{-x}})^{2} \\\\\n&= sech^{2} x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107121 #edb/ref 1065 5223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107121 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2a7389d5-4ba5-5fd7-8edb-b983483e6099" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107121 #edb/ref 1067 "Deriving the Formula for the Derivative of the Hyperbolic Tangent" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129089 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129089 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129089 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129089 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding Discontinuities Due to Vertical Asymptotes Using Graphs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129089 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129089 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e3170b49-74e4-540e-bd64-1be5e84605d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129090 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129090 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129090 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129090 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding Infinite Discontinuities of Rational Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129090 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129090 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "976f6e20-2c45-5123-bb60-738e099facc3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129091 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129091 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129091 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129091 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding Infinite Discontinuities of Rational Functions With Common Factors" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129091 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129091 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "ea5bdd3b-810c-56e6-acfd-4de0e0cfc4e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129092 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129092 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129092 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129092 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding Infinite Discontinuities of a Piecewise Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129092 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129092 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4317f5b8-cc60-5695-b5bb-47744cecc4cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129349 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186129905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129349 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186129906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129349 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186129907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129349 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186129908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129349 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186129909 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129349 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129349 #edb/ref 1045 "Discontinuities Due to Vertical Asymptotes" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129349 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186129905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129349 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129349 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "70c09bba-cfb5-5906-81b1-66e614d9a1e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129905 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129905 #edb/ref 1061 12112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129905 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "545e237d-8d82-5fad-8893-b53c598fe510" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129905 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129906 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129906 #edb/ref 1061 12113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129906 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8acbfdc6-b18f-5290-b574-bb7515ced757" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129906 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129907 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129907 #edb/ref 1061 12114 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129907 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bf945475-7921-5714-a012-fd5bfdd13e6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129907 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129908 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129909 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129908 #edb/ref 1061 12115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129908 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "18da17af-7589-58b8-ac89-6eded53c96a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129908 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129909 #edb/ref 1061 12116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129909 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "23a64c25-6876-5b6c-9e24-a82e1cee59ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129909 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130330 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's take a look at the function\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac 1 x\n$$\n\nbelow.\n\n![](images/cc/cc9593860b0c134d0d99635b37eeac4f16a712a7af9e1c6474fcf1f885e8451e.png)\n\nThe function has a vertical asymptote at $x=0$. There is also a discontinuity in the function at $x=0$, since we cannot draw the points on the curve without taking our pen off the paper to cross the asymptote.\n\nSo, we say that\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac 1 x\n$$\n\nhas a **discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote** at $x=0$. Discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes are also called **infinite discontinuities**.\n\nNotice that here, both one-sided limits are infinite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0^-} \\left(\\dfrac 1 x\\right) =-\\infty\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\\lim_{x\\to 0^+} \\left(\\dfrac 1 x \\right)=\\infty\n$$\n\nIn general, a function $y=f(x)$ has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=c$ if either one-sided limit is infinite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to c^-} f(x) =\\pm \\infty\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad\\lim_{x\\to c^+} f(x) =\\pm \\infty\n$$\n\nNote that we only need a single limit to become infinite to conclude that there is an infinite discontinuity at $x=c$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130330 #edb/ref 1065 2595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130330 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ce83f36f-d7f8-546f-b887-1e6818a75bfb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130330 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166865 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166865 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166865 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186167091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166865 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186167092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166865 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166865 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218616